Third-party and upstream material
---------------------------------

This repository is not a fork, and no inherited licence applies to it.

It was one, briefly. It began as a fork of anthropics/claude-code
<https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code>, that fork was deleted, and
the repository was recreated clean. The reason was that the upstream
holds no source for the CLI at all — it is a changelog, documentation,
plugins, examples and issues, and the artefact is the npm package — so
forking it bought nothing while carrying its issue-triage workflows and
its fork network's visibility along with it.

The consequence is recorded here because it is the kind of thing a
downstream recipient cannot verify for themselves years later: no line
of anthropics/claude-code survives in this tree. The Dockerfile, the
workflow and the documentation here were written for this project. There
is therefore no inherited MIT notice reproduced in this file, and none is
owed.

What this project writes is the recipe, not the contents. The Docker
image it publishes is, by volume, almost entirely software that this
project neither wrote nor distributes in source form here:

    - the Node base image, and the Debian userland underneath it;
    - @anthropic-ai/claude-code itself, installed unmodified from npm;
    - git, ripgrep, jq and less, from Debian.

Each of these is governed by its own terms, and those terms are the ones
shipped inside the image itself. They are aggregated with this program,
not derived from it: the AGPL covers this project's own work — the
Dockerfile, the workflow, the scripts and the documentation — and does
not, and could not, relicense any of the above. Building an AGPL recipe
around a piece of software does not place that software under the AGPL.

Claude Code itself remains subject to Anthropic's terms. This repository
hosts the unmodified binary and nothing more, which is what Anthropic
explicitly permits; installing it through this image grants no right that
Anthropic has not already granted, and takes none away either. Anyone
redistributing the image, or building a service on top of it, answers to
those terms directly and not to this project. In particular, routing
